diagnosis_text:
I tested the CS segment (192.168.2.0/24) end‑to‑end and specifically the gateway port you called out (router0 eth1 = 192.168.2.1). Multiple sustained pings from cs_lab1 to 192.168.2.1 and to the “Internet” segment (e.g., 128.168.0.2/…/0.5 behind router2) show 0% loss and steady sub‑3 ms RTTs. On router0 eth1, ethtool reports Link detected: yes, 10G/full, and interface stats show 0 errors/drops; on switch1, both eth0 (uplink to router0) and eth1 (CS hosts) are 10G/full and forwarding (STP) with clean counters. Traffic‑control (tc) stats on all involved ports report 0 drops.

Critically, link‑state counters show only a single flap since boot (router0 eth1: carrier_changes=2, up=1, down=1; switch1 eth0/eth1 the same). switch1 dmesg confirms a one‑time bring‑down/bring‑up during bridge formation at startup, not recurring link events. No ongoing “link down/up” messages appear in logs.

Mechanism: If LEDs were seen “blinking,” that’s normal activity indication, not link flaps. The momentary boot‑time bridge bring‑up could have caused a short outage during initial power‑on, but nothing is flapping now. Localization: router0 eth1 and switch1 uplink/CS ports are stable and forwarding.

Root cause: Misinterpretation of normal port LED activity and a one‑time startup transition; no failing gateway port.

Corrective fix: No port restart is required. If users still observe intermittent “freezes,” investigate endpoint causes (e.g., DNS/app delays) and consider adding continuous monitoring (smokeping) and enabling RIP passive-interface on LANs to reduce routing chatter, though it is not currently causing loss. 
=== END ===